5: /**
6:  * Notification realtime bridge for NotificationCenter (ALE-164).
7:  *
8:  * Replaces the Supabase-Realtime-backed bridge with a Phoenix-Session-backed
9:  * one. The browser can no longer read the HTTP-only `_dhc_session` cookie to
10:  * pass it as a Phoenix JS `authToken`, and `new WebSocket(url, protocols)`
11:  * has no `withCredentials` so a cross-origin socket cannot send the cookie.
12:  * Instead, the bridge fetches a short-lived, JS-readable socket token from
13:  * `GET /api/auth/socket-token` (credentialed, so the cookie is sent) and
14:  * passes it as `authToken`.
15:  *
16:  * Socket tokens are valid for 60 seconds, and Phoenix JS's own reconnect loop
17:  * reuses the original `authToken`, so the bridge owns recovery (ALE-337).
18:  * Sockets are constructed with a `reconnectAfterMs` that never fires in
19:  * practice, so Phoenix never reconnects by itself. When the live socket
20:  * errors or closes, the bridge leaves its channel, disconnects it, waits a
21:  * bounded backoff, fetches a fresh token, and connects a new socket. Every
22:  * successful (re)join calls `invalidate()` so events missed while
23:  * disconnected are recovered by an authoritative refetch, and resets the
24:  * backoff. A token fetch that hangs past a timeout is abandoned and retried;
25:  * a token that resolves after `destroy()` or after a newer attempt has
26:  * started is dropped. A retired socket that revives itself anyway (Phoenix's
27:  * heartbeat-timeout or `pageshow` paths) is disconnected again on its next
28:  * lifecycle event.
